\section{Temperature Robustness Pilot}
\label{app:temperature}

All main experiments use temperature $= 0$. To assess robustness under stochastic sampling, we conduct a pilot on GPT-4.1 with 50 randomly sampled clause pairs (100 episodes per condition).

\paragraph{Design.} Three conditions: (1)~temperature $= 0$ explicitly set ($n = 100$); (2)~temperature $= 0.7$ with five independent runs ($n = 500$); (3)~API default with no temperature parameter ($n = 20$, validating original experiment settings). All episodes are judged by GPT-5.4.

\paragraph{API default validation.} Episodes run without an explicit temperature parameter match the temperature $= 0$ condition in 90.0\% of cases (18/20), confirming that the original experiment effectively operated at temperature $= 0$.

\paragraph{Treatment effect.} The ambiguity treatment effect is robust across temperatures: $\Delta = +34.0$\,pp at temperature $= 0$ vs.\ $\Delta = +31.2$\,pp (SD $= 5.0$) at temperature $= 0.7$ (range $[+28.0, +40.0]$ across five runs). All five runs produce significant positive effects.

\paragraph{Clause-level agreement.} At temperature $= 0.7$, 83.0\% of clause$\times$condition pairs produce unanimous outcomes across all five runs; 93.0\% agree at $\geq$4/5. Comparing temperature $= 0$ to the temperature $= 0.7$ majority vote, 97.0\% of clause$\times$condition pairs produce identical violation outcomes, indicating that temperature primarily introduces noise around a stable underlying signal rather than altering which clauses trigger violations.

